.TH HD-KERNEL-VM 7 "November 2026" "hd-kernel-vm" "HyprDebian"
.SH NAME
hd-kernel-vm \- the HyprDebian kernel for KVM virtual machines
.SH DESCRIPTION
The package
.B hd-kernel-vm
depends on the most recent HyprDebian kernel for virtual machines of the
suite and its ZFS module, so that they are installed and upgraded with it:
.TP
.BI linux-image- release
The kernel of
.BR hd-kernel (7):
the kernel of Debian, with the patches and the config of Debian, built by the
build server of HyprDebian, but with only the modules that a KVM virtual
machine needs.
.TP
.BI zfs-modules- release
The ZFS module of the
.B zfs-linux
of Debian, built against that kernel, in
.IR /lib/modules/ release /extra/zfs .
It provides
.BR zfs-modules ,
which
.B zfs-initramfs
takes in place of
.BR zfs-dkms .
.PP
What the config of Debian builds into the kernel stays, and so do the modules
that are not drivers: the file systems, the network protocols and netfilter,
crypto, and kvm, for virtual machines in the virtual machine. Of the drivers
only these stay:
.IP \(bu 2
those of the hardware that QEMU emulates for a KVM virtual machine: virtio for
disks, network, graphics, sound, input, memory and random numbers; SATA, IDE,
NVMe and SCSI disks and cd-roms; the network cards e1000, e1000e and RTL8139;
the USB controllers with keyboards, mice, tablets, storage and audio; the
standard VGA, QXL and Cirrus graphics; HDA and AC97 sound; the power button
of ACPI, by which the host shuts the virtual machine down; the SMBus, LPC and
watchdog of the q35 chipset, the watchdogs i6300esb and ib700, and the PC
speaker; fw_cfg, pvpanic and the KVM clock for PTP;
.IP \(bu 2
those of software: loop, zram and nbd, device mapper and RAID, and the network
devices of containers, virtual machines, VPNs and bonds, such as tun, veth,
macvlan, vxlan, wireguard and vhost.
.PP
The headers of each kernel, for DKMS, are in
.BI linux-headers- release\fR,
which no package depends on; to build modules for the kernel that runs:
.PP
.RS
sudo apt install linux-headers-$(uname -r)
.RE
.PP
The release is
.IB x.y.z +debNN-hdvm id -amd64\fR,
such as
.BR 7.2.12+deb14-hdvm1a2b3c-amd64 ,
where
.I id
comes from the versions of
.BR linux ,
.B zfs-linux
and
.BR hd-kernel-vm .
A new build has a new release, so it installs next to the kernel that runs.
.PP
Every build signs the modules of the kernel and the ZFS module with a key it
makes for the build and then throws away; the kernel trusts that key and no
other for its modules.
.SH SWITCHING
To switch a virtual machine to the HyprDebian kernel for virtual machines:
.PP
.RS
sudo apt install hd-kernel-vm
.RE
.PP
This removes the other HyprDebian kernels,
.BR hd-kernel ,
.B hd-kernel-server
and
.BR hd-kernel-desktop ,
and
.BR linux-image-amd64 ,
.B linux-headers-amd64
and
.BR linux-base-amd64 ,
so the other kernels get no more upgrades.
After the installation the bootloader shows the new kernel at the top of its
menu, with
.B -hd
in its name, see
.BR hd-bootloader-update (8).
Once it boots, the other kernels can be purged, as
.BR hd-kernel (7)
describes for the kernels of Debian.
.PP
To switch back to the kernel of
.BR hd-kernel ,
or to that of Debian:
.PP
.RS
sudo apt install hd-kernel
.br
sudo apt install linux-image-amd64 linux-headers-amd64
.RE
.PP
Either removes
.BR hd-kernel-vm ;
its kernels stay until they are purged.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR /boot/vmlinuz- release
.TQ
.IR /lib/modules/ release
The kernel and its modules.
.TP
.IR /lib/modules/ release /extra/zfs
The ZFS module.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hd-bootloader-update (8),
.BR hd-buildserver (8),
.BR hd-kernel (7),
.BR hd-kernel-desktop (7),
.BR hd-kernel-server (7),
.BR zfs (4)
